            {
              "id": "art-body",
              "type": "column",
              "props": {
                "span": 9
              },
              "children": [
                {
                  "id": "lede",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If your semi truck has started losing power, hesitating under load, or becoming harder to start, the problem may not be your engine. It could be something much simpler: your fuel filters.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full",
                    "anchor": "post-lede"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-intro-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Maintaining proper fuel filter pressure in your semi truck is essential for keeping your engine running efficiently. Fuel filters are responsible for removing dirt, debris, water, and contaminants before they reach sensitive fuel system components. For owner-operators and fleet managers, understanding how fuel filter pressure affects performance can help prevent unexpected downtime and extend the life of your truck.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-how-it-affects",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "How Fuel Filter Pressure Can Affect Your Engine",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "how-it-affects"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-how-it-affects-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Modern diesel engines rely on consistent fuel pressure to operate efficiently. When fuel cannot flow freely through the filter, the entire fuel system is affected.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-how-it-affects-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "A restricted filter can cause:",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "checks-how-it-affects-3",
                  "type": "check-list",
                  "props": {
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Reduced engine power"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Poor fuel economy"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Hard starting"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Rough idle"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Loss of acceleration"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Increased strain on the fuel pump"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-how-it-affects-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If ignored for too long, low fuel pressure can damage expensive components like injectors and high-pressure fuel pumps. Replacing filters before they become restricted is one of the simplest ways to avoid major repairs.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-causes",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "What Causes Fuel Filter Pressure Problems?",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "causes"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-causes-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Fuel filters naturally collect contaminants over time. That's exactly what they're designed to do. However, once enough debris builds up, fuel flow becomes restricted.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-causes-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Common causes include:",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "checks-causes-3",
                  "type": "check-list",
                  "props": {
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Dirty fuel"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Water contamination"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Normal filter wear"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Poor maintenance intervals"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Biological growth inside fuel tanks"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Sediment from aging tanks"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-causes-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Florida's heat and humidity can also contribute to fuel contamination if tanks aren't maintained properly. Following a consistent diesel truck maintenance schedule helps catch these issues before they lead to breakdowns.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-primary-secondary",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Understanding Primary and Secondary Fuel Filters",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "primary-secondary"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-primary-secondary-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Many heavy-duty diesel engines use two fuel filters.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-primary-secondary-2",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Semi Truck Pre-Filters",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "pre-filters"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-primary-secondary-3",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The first stage is often referred to as the semi-truck pre-filter or primary fuel filter. Its job is to remove larger contaminants and separate water before fuel reaches the engine. Replacing this filter regularly protects the rest of the fuel system from unnecessary wear.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-primary-secondary-4",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Secondary Fuel Filters",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "secondary-filters"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-primary-secondary-5",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "The secondary filter captures much finer particles before fuel reaches the injectors. Because today's diesel injection systems operate under extremely high pressure, even microscopic contaminants can cause expensive damage.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-primary-secondary-6",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Replacing both filters at the recommended intervals helps maintain proper fuel filter pressure in your semi truck while protecting critical engine components.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-primary-secondary-7",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Need Replacement Fuel Filters?",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "need-filters"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-primary-secondary-8",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Sun State International stocks quality OEM and aftermarket semi truck oil filters, fuel filters, and other maintenance parts to keep your fleet operating at peak performance. Visit our Parts Department to find the right filters for your truck.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "co-primary-secondary-9-row",
                  "type": "row",
                  "props": {
                    "gap": 0
                  },
                  "children": [
                    {
